## This Month in Recalls: A Hot Mess

June hasn't been kind to consumers seeking safety in their food choices. According to the latest data, there were 43 recalls reported so far this month, contributing to a concerning total of 543 food recalls this year alone. A significant uptick in May (173 recalls) suggests a worrying trend as we head through the summer.

## The Usual Suspects

Let's talk about the worst of the worst. JCB Flavors and Straus Family Creamery have made a splash for all the wrong reasons. JCB Flavors, with their seasoning products like White Cheddar Seasoning and Garlic N Cheese Topper, have been implicated in multiple Class I recalls due to potential Salmonella contamination. Straus, on the other hand, managed to let foreign objects slip into their organic ice cream products. Mint Chip or Cookie Dough, anyone?

## A Lack of Accountability?

Repeat offenders remain a significant issue, with 86 brands failing to learn from past mistakes. Moonlight Packing Corporation, a repeat offender, leads the pack with an abysmal score of 96 out of 100 ("F" grade) due to 11 recalls, all categorized as Class I hazards. Why are these brands still on store shelves?

## What Does This Mean for You?

The numbers are alarming: 173 Class I recalls this year alone. Salmonella leads the charge in contamination risks, accounting for 172 recalls. Listeria and allergens also represent significant hazards. It's clear that consumers need to remain vigilant, stay informed, and demand better transparency and safety from food manufacturers.

## Closing Thoughts

As more cases appear each month, the spotlight remains firmly placed on those responsible. The responsibility falls not only on the shoulders of individual brands but also on regulators and consumers to ensure food safety standards are upheld.

Stay safe out there, and keep reading Shelf Check for your daily dose of food recall updates.
